WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:13.170
Ever been bothered by an ultra-slow download?

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:16.960
How about a phone that won’t stay charged
or a cracked touchscreen?

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:20.730
Imagine you were stranded on an island with
nothing to drink.

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:24.560
What if science came up with a way to solve
all these problems with a single chemical

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:28.010
compound that you can find, of all places,
in a pencil?

00:00:28.010 --> 00:00:30.820
A thousand gigabytes in one second.

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:33.370
A smartphone fully charged in 5.

00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:37.190
Superthin, flexible, and unbreakable touchscreens.

00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:39.720
And a personal saltwater filter.

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:44.750
All this and more is possible with the Nobel
prize-winning material graphene.

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:49.410
As early as the 1940s, theoretical studies
predicted graphene to have high levels of

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:54.130
electrical conductivity, thermal conductivity,
and breakage strength.

00:00:54.130 --> 00:00:58.921
Graphene was also known to compose the subunits
of the common material graphite, found in

00:00:58.921 --> 00:01:00.190
a pencil.

00:01:00.190 --> 00:01:02.870
Draw a line and groups of graphene flakes,
comprised of carbon atoms arranged in a honeycomb

00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:05.820
array, slide onto the paper, producing a mark.

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:10.640
The problem was that no one could figure out
how to isolate a single flake.

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:15.760
Scientists tried various chemical means, resulting
in nothing more than graphitic sludge.

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:21.340
Mechanical polishing got them to 0.001 mm
of graphite, but that was still about 1000

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:22.590
layers of graphene.

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:29.220
In 2002, a group from the University of Manchester
took a more “MacGyverist” approach.

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:34.770
Using their self-dubbed “Scotch-tape method”,
physicists Sir Andre Geim and Sir Konstantin

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:40.100
Novoselov peeled away at a graphite crystal,
producing flakes that became vanishingly thin.

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:43.350
An atomic force microscope revealed their
success.

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:48.850
The elusive material finally in hand, scientists
were able to see just what graphene could

00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:49.959
do.

00:01:49.959 --> 00:01:53.509
As predicted, it’s an excellent electrical
conductor.

00:01:53.509 --> 00:01:59.020
The free electron on each carbon atom in graphene
enables movement of charge across the surface.

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:04.970
Moreover, it’s thermal conductivity, or
ability to transfer heat, is higher than that

00:02:04.970 --> 00:02:06.100
of copper.

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:11.480
Mechanically, the strong bonds between carbon
atoms and the inherent stability of a hexagonal

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:15.790
array combine to produce the strongest material
known to man.

00:02:15.790 --> 00:02:20.739
Nonetheless, the bonds of graphene retain
remarkable flexibility.

00:02:20.739 --> 00:02:25.489
As a sprayable, two-dimensional surface, it
can be used to easily tune the properties

00:02:25.489 --> 00:02:26.829
of other compounds.

00:02:26.829 --> 00:02:32.150
A lot of people are excited about graphene’s
possible versatile applications.

00:02:32.150 --> 00:02:37.319
Because graphene has very high conductivity
and is almost completely transparent, it could

00:02:37.319 --> 00:02:40.489
be used to make more efficient electronics.

00:02:40.489 --> 00:02:45.659
These electronics have the potential to be
more environmentally safe once graphene extraction

00:02:45.659 --> 00:02:47.579
techniques are improved.

00:02:47.579 --> 00:02:52.409
With its high breakage strength, graphene
could be the key to lighter and stronger building

00:02:52.409 --> 00:02:53.769
materials.

00:02:53.769 --> 00:02:58.849
New techniques for creating nano-scale pores
in sheets of graphene indicate that it could

00:02:58.849 --> 00:03:01.500
be used to make ultra-thin filters.

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:06.190
As scientists think up new ways to use this
supermaterial, it’s worth recalling its

00:03:06.190 --> 00:03:08.959
humble entry into the scientific world.

00:03:08.959 --> 00:03:13.859
Using a piece of scotch tape and a block of
pencil lead, two scientists were able to make

00:03:13.859 --> 00:03:15.620
a groundbreaking discovery.

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:22.060
In 2010, the Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences
awarded Geim and Novoselov the Nobel Prize

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:23.810
in Physics for their efforts.

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:30.189
Together, they proved that scientific innovation
does not always require fancy instrumentation;

00:03:30.189 --> 00:03:32.170
it requires persistence and creativity.

